diagnosis_text:
No network fault is present, and the super-spine is not black-holing traffic. End-to-end tests show the fabric is forwarding normally: a topology-wide reachability check and a direct ping from pc_0_0 to pc_0_1 both succeed with 0% loss and ~1.7 ms RTT. A traceroute from pc_0_0 to 10.0.1.2 shows the path 10.0.0.1 (leaf_router_0_0) -> 172.16.0.4 (spine_router_0_0) -> 172.16.0.7 (spine_router_0_1) -> 10.0.1.2, confirming the flow never traverses super_spine_router_0. This matches the control plane: FRR routing tables on the leaves and spines have correct BGP routes to 10.0.0.0/24 and 10.0.1.0/24 via their directly connected leaf/spine links, and the super-spine only learns both /24s via 172.16.0.1 (its uplinks), not used for intra-leaf communication.

Mechanism: In this 2-stage CLOS, east-west traffic between leaf_router_0_0 and leaf_router_0_1 is carried by the spines; the super-spine is upstream and not on the data path for this flow. Given the successful pings and trace, there is no evidence of packet drops or black-holing at any stage.

Localization: All observed forwarding is correct on leaf_router_0_0, spine_router_0_0, spine_router_0_1, and leaf_router_0_1. The super_spine is not involved in the path.

Root cause: The reported outage is not reproducible; likely a transient or non-network issue.

Corrective fix: Do not restart any device. If the issue reappears, capture simultaneous ping/traceroute and host firewall/ARP state during the event. 
